-- §3b  The same classes, counted over the corpus and over the fixtures instead of pooled.
# One row per (relation, class) of every classification whose rows are filed: what the documents
# attesting to a real system put there, and what the documents stipulating a state put there.
#
# The two numbers answer different questions, and the pooled one answers neither
#   A class empty in the corpus and filled in the fixtures is a state this repository invented
#   because nothing real produced it: a finding about the evidence. A class empty in the fixtures
#   is a state no stipulation exercises: a gap in coverage. Added together they say only that
#   somebody somewhere filed one, which is the question `epistemics/classes.sqlc` answers for the
#   contract and the only question pooling can answer honestly.
#
# Why this could not be a filter over §3
#   §3 counts, so the set of documents it counted over is part of its result rather than an
#   input a reader still holds. Restricting its rows afterwards chooses which totals to look
#   at, never what each total counted. Both columns here come from `reports/class_census.sqlc`,
#   which takes the document set as a slot and is composed twice.
#
# Two classifications are absent, and that is their subject rather than an omission
#   `checks/fit_axes.sqlc` and `checks/fit_domain.sqlc` classify this tree's own RULES, which
#   nobody files, so their classes are identical under every scope and a zero here would read as
#   a gap in evidence that does not exist.
-- reports/class_census.sqlc at both scopes, matched on the class.
SELECT k.relation AS "relation!", k.class AS "class!", k.subject AS "subject?",
       k.balls AS "corpus!", f.balls AS "fixtures?"
FROM      (
    :compose(reports/classes_in_the_corpus.sqlc)
) k
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(reports/classes_in_the_fixtures.sqlc)
) f ON f.relation = k.relation AND f.class = k.class
ORDER BY k.relation, k.ord
